        "videos/18-thought-to-text/G4.jpg"
      ],
      "imageAt": [
        0,
        0.455
      ],
      "seconds": 22.24,
      "text": "Speech starts as a motor plan. Before a single sound leaves you, your brain is already issuing instructions to the lips, jaw and tongue. In paralysis, that plan is often still intact. It just never reaches the muscles. So two teams placed electrodes over those regions and trained models to read the instructions directly. Attempted speech, decoded into text, in close to real time.",
      "delivery": {
        "rate": 8,
        "pitch": 0
      },
      "clips": [
        {
          "src": "videos/18-thought-to-text/v2-1.mp3",
